WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.950
Hello world, in this video I'll be
introducing interval notation and talk

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:09.000
about some common sets. This will lay the foundation for the next video on set

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.440
builder notation. The beauty of interval
notation is that allows us to officially

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:19.320
described all numbers between two values.
Suppose we want to say that the variable

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:22.500
X is between Zero and 1.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.849
Well, we could do just that we can say
that X is an element of the

00:00:26.849 --> 00:00:33.630
interval Zero, 1. Or, which is really saying X
is greater than Zero and X is less than 1.

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:38.399
Likewise, if we wanted to say that X is
greater than or equal to Zero and X is less

00:00:38.399 --> 00:00:43.020
than 1 then we have to switch this to a
square bracket. All we're doing is we're

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:49.110
now including the number Zero in the
interval. Also we can include Zero and we

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:53.850
can also include 1 in the interval by using
square brackets on both sides. So it's

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:59.340
greater than or equal and
less than or equal to. And now talk about

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:02.789
some common sets, the first one being the
null set, which is the same thing as the

00:01:02.789 --> 00:01:08.700
empty set, which is just there are no
elements contained within the set and

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:14.850
this will come in handy future. The funny
N here is the natural numbers, which is

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:20.610
the set containing elements 1, 2, 3, and then
these thre e dots here called an ellipsis,

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:26.159
which tells us that the pattern
continues. So, N is the set containing

00:01:26.159 --> 00:01:34.290
1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7 all the way up to infinity. This funny N with a Zero is the natural

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:41.820
numbers, however it includes the value
Zero within the set. So it's 0, 1, 2 in the

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:46.380
ellipsis tells us all the way to
infinity in an integer Zero, 1, 2, 3, 4,

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:51.090
5, 6. And finally, we have all the
integers positive and negative and non

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:57.630
which is Zero, negative 1, negative 2 all the
way down to negative infinity, and Zero, 1, 2,

00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:59.430
3, 4, all the way up to infinity.

